redraw only what changed in the menu

The in-game menu was still at 30 fps on 0049, with blue light on: 16.8-18.4
ms of overlay on every frame it changed. Two things were left.

Every change -- the cursor moving a row -- recomposed and copied out the
whole rect of the overlay, and with a scrim over nearly all of the canvas
that is nearly all of the canvas: 1.5 MB copied from the kept picture into
the canvas, read again to reverse it, and written to the panel, for two
highlight rows. On the Flip's memory that is most of the frame. Over a
still game every page already holds the last overlay, so a change now
marks only what changed: the sprites in one list and not the other, and
the rows a scrim gained or lost, which for a scrim that kept its colour is
only where its edges moved. Of the overlay's sprites, only those reaching
into those rows are drawn. With the game moving, or after a repaint,
nothing changes from before.

0049's pre-tinted copies did not hold: the labels still drew at 79-104 ns
a pixel, the copies rebuilt as the textures were. A tinted blit now builds
a 256-entry table a channel per draw instead -- `modulate`'s bytes, with
nothing kept that can go stale -- and the copies are gone.

Host, release, blue light on, menu cursor moving every 4th frame: 0.107
ms a frame before, 0.050 after. The host's memory hides most of what the
first change saves; not yet measured on the Flip.

Tests: the scrim's edge sliding over a still game with a label riding on
it, against the canvas path every frame, both ways up; a cursor move marks
under 100 rows changed; a tinted blit matches the reference at every scale
and clip under three tints. Leaving out the scrim's moved rows fails three
tests, leaving out sprites that went fails three, and a table built from
the wrong channel fails four.

Applies on top of 0049, upstream 91499a9.

